        content: `<p>Commercial property is, by default, exempt from VAT. This means a landlord letting or selling commercial property does not charge VAT, and equally cannot reclaim any VAT incurred on costs related to that property (such as building work, professional fees, or the purchase itself). For many simple commercial property transactions, the VAT position is therefore neutral and straightforward.</p>
<p>However, the majority of commercial landlords elect to <strong>opt to tax</strong> their property. An option to tax is an election made with HMRC that converts an otherwise VAT-exempt supply into a standard-rated supply at 20%. Once a landlord has opted to tax a property, all supplies related to that property — rental income, the sale of the freehold — become subject to VAT. The landlord can then recover all VAT incurred on costs related to that property.</p>
<p>For SIPP investors, the key practical question is: when you buy a commercial property, has the seller opted to tax? If they have, you will need to pay VAT on the purchase price in addition to the headline price — a 20% uplift that must be funded.</p>`,
